---
// The State of CNC Relief Carving 2026.
//
// This page exists to be CITED, not to sell. It is the one thing on this site
// nobody else can publish: 365 days of real sales across a 1,226 design
// catalogue, with the method stated plainly enough that a sceptic can attack it.
//
// Two rules were followed while building it and both matter more than the
// design. First, the figures are BAKED IN at build time from a checked-in JSON
// snapshot, so a number a journalist quotes today still reads the same in a
// year. Second, the maturity control (see the method section) throws away 776
// designs that are too new to have sold; without it the catalogue appears to
// have a 55% failure rate and a spectacular fake finding about title length.
import Shell from '../../layouts/Shell.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import data from '../../../scripts/blog/state-of-cnc-relief-carving-2026/report_data.json';
import { jsonLd } from '../../lib/jsonld';

const { concentration: C, subjects, priceBands, categories, cohort } = data as any;
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
const PUB = '2026-09-22';

// Charts are hand-built SVG rather than a library: one dependency fewer, and a
// bar chart is twelve lines of geometry. Each bar carries its value as a real
// label, which is also what discharges the palette's contrast relief rule.
const subjectsByRate = [...subjects].filter((s: any) => s.designs >= 20).sort((a: any, b: any) => b.perDesign - a.perDesign);
const maxPerDesign = Math.max(...subjectsByRate.map((s: any) => s.perDesign));
const bands = priceBands.filter((b: any) => b.designs >= 10);
const maxBand = Math.max(...bands.map((b: any) => b.perDesign));
const cats = categories.slice(0, 12);
const maxCat = Math.max(...cats.map((c: any) => c.perDesign));

const ld = jsonLd({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Report',
  headline: 'The State of CNC Relief Carving 2026',
  datePublished: PUB,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'DigitalChiselCo' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'DigitalChiselCo' },
  description: `An analysis of ${fmt(C.sales)} real sales across ${fmt(C.designs)} CNC relief carving designs over 365 days.`,
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
});
---
<Shell
  title="The State of CNC Relief Carving 2026 · DigitalChiselCo"
  description={`We analysed ${fmt(C.sales)} real sales across ${fmt(C.designs)} relief carving designs over 365 days. What subjects sell, what price actually does to demand, and how concentrated the market really is.`}
  fullWidth={true}
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={ld} />
  <div class="rpt">
    <div class="rpt-wrap">
      <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Reports', href: '/reports' }, { label: 'State of CNC Relief Carving 2026' }]} />

      <header class="rpt-hero">
        <p class="rpt-eyebrow">First-party data · {data.window}</p>
        <h1>The State of CNC Relief Carving 2026</h1>
        <p class="rpt-lede">
          Everybody in this trade argues about what sells. Almost nobody has the numbers, because the
          shops that have them do not publish them. We do, so here they are: <strong>{fmt(C.sales)} real sales</strong>
          across <strong>{fmt(C.designs)} designs</strong> over a full year, with the method written out so you can
          decide for yourself how much to trust it.
        </p>
        <p class="rpt-sub">
          Free to quote, chart and republish with a link back. No signup, no email wall.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section class="rpt-tiles" aria-label="Headline figures">
        <div class="tile"><div class="tile-n">{fmt(C.designs)}</div><div class="tile-l">designs in the study</div></div>
        <div class="tile"><div class="tile-n">{fmt(C.sales)}</div><div class="tile-l">sales over 365 days</div></div>
        <div class="tile"><div class="tile-n">{Math.round(100 * C.neverSold / C.designs)}%</div><div class="tile-l">never sold once</div></div>
        <div class="tile"><div class="tile-n">{C.top10pcShare}%</div><div class="tile-l">of sales go to the top 10%</div></div>
      </section>

      <!-- ── 1 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section class="rpt-sec">
        <h2 id="concentration">1. Most designs sell nothing, and that is normal</h2>
        <p>
          Of {fmt(C.designs)} designs that have been on sale for a full year, <strong>{fmt(C.neverSold)} never sold a single copy</strong>.
          The top {C.topHalfCount} designs, {(100 * C.topHalfCount / C.designs).toFixed(1)}% of the catalogue, account for half of all sales.
          The top 10% account for {C.top10pcShare}%.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is the number that should change how you think about building a catalogue. A designer measuring
          themselves by hit rate will conclude they are failing. They are not. In a catalogue this size a
          {' '}<strong>{Math.round(100 * C.neverSold / C.designs)}% miss rate is the cost of finding the {C.topHalfCount} that carry everything.</strong>
          {' '}The implication is uncomfortable for anyone selling a "proven winners" pack: the winners are only
          visible afterwards.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ── 2 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section class="rpt-sec">
        <h2 id="price">2. The expensive designs sell more, not fewer</h2>
        <p>
          This is the finding that surprised us most, and the one we expect to be argued with hardest.
          Designs priced at $15 to $20 average <strong>{bands.find((b: any) => b.band === '$15 to $20')?.perDesign} sales each</strong>.
          Designs priced at $5 to $8 average <strong>{bands.find((b: any) => b.band === '$5 to $8')?.perDesign}</strong>.
          That is the cheap tier selling roughly a fifth as often per design, at a third of the price.
        </p>

        <figure class="chart">
          <figcaption>Average sales per design, by price band</figcaption>
          <div class="bars">
            {bands.map((b: any) => (
              <div class="row" title={`${b.band}: ${b.perDesign} sales per design across ${b.designs} designs, ${b.hitRate}% of them sold at least once`}>
                <div class="row-l">{b.band}</div>
                <div class="row-track">
                  <div class="row-bar" style={`width:${Math.max(2, 86 * b.perDesign / maxBand)}%`}></div>
                  <span class="row-v">{b.perDesign}</span>
                </div>
                <div class="row-n">{b.designs} designs</div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </figure>

        <p class="caveat">
          <strong>Read this one carefully.</strong> Price is not causing the sales. The expensive designs are
          the large, complex, multi-figure scenes that took the longest to make, and the cheap tier is where
          almost the whole catalogue sits ({bands.find((b: any) => b.band === '$5 to $8')?.designs} of {fmt(C.designs)} designs).
          The honest claim is narrower than "raise your prices": <strong>pricing low did not buy volume.</strong>
          The cheap tier holds {bands.find((b: any) => b.band === '$5 to $8')?.revenueShare}% of revenue while
          holding {Math.round(100 * bands.find((b: any) => b.band === '$5 to $8')?.designs / C.designs)}% of the catalogue.
          The $15 to $20 band is 20 designs, so treat it as a direction, not a decimal.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ── 3 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section class="rpt-sec">
        <h2 id="subjects">3. What people actually carve</h2>
        <p>
          Subjects are matched on listing titles, and a design can belong to more than one, so these describe
          demand for a theme rather than slices of a pie. Sorted by sales per design, which is the number that
          matters if you are deciding what to make next, rather than by total sales, which mostly tells you what
          a shop happened to make a lot of.
        </p>

        <figure class="chart">
          <figcaption>Average sales per design, by subject (subjects with 20+ designs)</figcaption>
          <div class="bars">
            {subjectsByRate.map((s: any) => (
              <div class="row" title={`${s.name}: ${s.perDesign} sales per design, ${s.hitRate}% sold at least once, average price $${s.avgPrice}`}>
                <div class="row-l">{s.name}</div>
                <div class="row-track">
                  <div class="row-bar" style={`width:${Math.max(2, 86 * s.perDesign / maxPerDesign)}%`}></div>
                  <span class="row-v">{s.perDesign}</span>
                </div>
                <div class="row-n">{s.hitRate}% sold</div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Religious work is the largest single block by total volume at {subjects.find((s: any) => s.name === 'Religious')?.share}% of all sales,
          and it also carries the highest average price at ${subjects.find((s: any) => s.name === 'Religious')?.avgPrice}.
          But deer and elk is the most reliable thing in the catalogue per design.
          Nautical has the highest hit rate of any subject at {subjects.find((s: any) => s.name === 'Nautical and sea life')?.hitRate}%:
          almost everything in it sells something.
        </p>
        <p>
          The quiet lesson is in the gap between volume and rate. Floral and botanical has
          {' '}{subjects.find((s: any) => s.name === 'Floral and botanical')?.designs} designs producing
          {' '}{subjects.find((s: any) => s.name === 'Floral and botanical')?.perDesign} sales each.
          Deer and elk has {subjects.find((s: any) => s.name === 'Deer and elk')?.designs} producing
          {' '}{subjects.find((s: any) => s.name === 'Deer and elk')?.perDesign}. Making more of the first category
          feels productive and is not.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ── 4 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section class="rpt-sec">
        <h2 id="categories">4. The same picture by shop category</h2>
        <p>Categories are the shop's own taxonomy rather than title matching, so this is a second, independent cut of the same year.</p>
        <figure class="chart">
          <figcaption>Average sales per design, by category (categories with 15+ designs, top 12)</figcaption>
          <div class="bars">
            {cats.map((c: any) => (
              <div class="row" title={`${c.name}: ${c.perDesign} sales per design across ${c.designs} designs`}>
                <div class="row-l">{c.name}</div>
                <div class="row-track">
                  <div class="row-bar alt" style={`width:${Math.max(2, 86 * c.perDesign / maxCat)}%`}></div>
                  <span class="row-v">{c.perDesign}</span>
                </div>
                <div class="row-n">{c.hitRate}% sold</div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- ── method ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section class="rpt-sec method">
        <h2 id="method">Method, and what we threw away</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>The data.</strong> Every figure comes from one shop's own sales records over
          {' '}{data.window}. Sales counts are per design. No buyer, order or personal detail is used anywhere
          in this report, and nothing below the aggregate level is published.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The maturity control, which is the important part.</strong> The raw catalogue contains
          {' '}{fmt(cohort.excluded)} designs that were excluded, almost all of them because they were added in the last
          few months and have not had a year to sell. Including them would have produced a much more dramatic
          report: a 55% failure rate instead of {Math.round(100 * C.neverSold / C.designs)}%, and a striking
          finding that listing titles over 90 characters sell nothing at all. Both are artefacts. The long titles
          belong to the new designs. We only study designs that were on sale before
          {' '}{cohort.rule.replace('listed on Etsy and created before ', '')}.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What this is not.</strong> This is one shop, selling digital files, mostly to buyers in
          English-speaking countries, on one marketplace. It describes that demand well and it should not be
          read as the whole world market. Subject buckets overlap by design. Bands with few designs are labelled
          with their counts so you can discount them yourself.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Corrections.</strong> If you find an error, say so and we will fix it and note the change here.
          A report nobody can check is marketing, not data.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="rpt-sec cite">
        <h2 id="cite">Use this</h2>
        <p>
          Free to reproduce, including the charts, for any purpose including commercial, with a link back to
          this page. No permission needed and no email required.
        </p>
        <p class="cite-line">
          <em>The State of CNC Relief Carving 2026</em>, DigitalChiselCo. https://digitalchiselco.com/reports/state-of-cnc-relief-carving-2026
        </p>
        <p>
          Questions about the method, or want a cut of the data we did not publish?
          {' '}<a href="/contact">Ask</a>. If you run a shop and want to pool anonymised numbers for next
          year's edition so it stops being one shop's view, that is exactly what we want to do.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Shell>

<style is:global>
  /* Chart colours are the brand bronze plus one validated contrast hue, and
     both modes were checked with the palette validator rather than by eye:
     light #854F0B / #2a78d6, dark #BE7F31 / #3987e5, all checks pass. Every
     bar also carries a printed value, which is what makes the chart readable
     without colour at all. */
  .rpt {
    --rpt-surface: #FFFDF8;
    --rpt-card: #ffffff;
    --rpt-ink: #2b1d10;
    --rpt-ink-2: #5c4a38;
    --rpt-line: rgba(43, 29, 16, .14);
    --rpt-bar: #854F0B;
    --rpt-bar-alt: #2a78d6;
    background: var(--rpt-surface);
    color: var(--rpt-ink);
  }
  /* Deliberately single-theme. The storefront around this page is light only,
     so a dark report would render as a dark island between a light header and
     a light footer. The dark steps (#BE7F31 / #3987e5) are validated and kept
     in the note above for whenever the rest of the site grows a dark mode. */

  .rpt-wrap { max-width: 48rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1rem 5rem; }
  .rpt-hero { padding: 2rem 0 1rem; }
  .rpt-eyebrow { font-size: .75rem; letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--rpt-ink-2); margin: 0 0 .75rem; }
  .rpt-hero h1 { font-family: Fraunces, Georgia, serif; font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 5vw, 3rem); line-height: 1.08; text-wrap: balance; margin: 0 0 1rem; color: var(--rpt-ink); }
  .rpt-lede { font-size: 1.125rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--rpt-ink); margin: 0 0 .75rem; }
  .rpt-sub { font-size: .9rem; color: var(--rpt-ink-2); margin: 0; }

  .rpt-tiles { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: .75rem; margin: 2rem 0 1rem; }
  @media (min-width: 720px) { .rpt-tiles { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); } }
  .tile { background: var(--rpt-card); border: 1px solid var(--rpt-line); border-radius: .6rem; padding: 1rem; }
  .tile-n { font-family: Fraunces, Georgia, serif; font-size: 1.9rem; line-height: 1; color: var(--rpt-bar); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .tile-l { font-size: .8rem; color: var(--rpt-ink-2); margin-top: .4rem; line-height: 1.35; }

  .rpt-sec { margin: 3rem 0 0; }
  .rpt-sec h2 { font-family: Fraunces, Georgia, serif; font-size: 1.6rem; line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 .9rem; color: var(--rpt-ink); text-wrap: balance; }
  .rpt-sec p { line-height: 1.7; margin: 0 0 1rem; color: var(--rpt-ink); }
  .rpt-sec a { color: var(--rpt-bar); text-decoration: underline; }
  .caveat { background: var(--rpt-card); border-left: 3px solid var(--rpt-bar); border-radius: .3rem; padding: .9rem 1rem; font-size: .95rem; }
  .method p, .cite p { font-size: .95rem; }
  .cite-line { background: var(--rpt-card); border: 1px solid var(--rpt-line); border-radius: .4rem; padding: .75rem .9rem; font-size: .85rem; word-break: break-word; }

  .chart { margin: 1.5rem 0 1.25rem; padding: 1rem; background: var(--rpt-card); border: 1px solid var(--rpt-line); border-radius: .6rem; }
  .chart figcaption { font-size: .85rem; color: var(--rpt-ink-2); margin-bottom: .9rem; }
  .bars { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .5rem; }
  .row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 8.5rem 1fr 5rem; align-items: center; gap: .6rem; }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: .15rem; }
    .row-n { text-align: left; }
  }
  .row-l { font-size: .82rem; color: var(--rpt-ink); line-height: 1.25; }
  .row-track { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; min-width: 0; }
  .row-bar { height: 14px; background: var(--rpt-bar); border-radius: 0 4px 4px 0; flex: 0 0 auto; }
  .row-bar.alt { background: var(--rpt-bar-alt); }
  .row-v { font-size: .8rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; color: var(--rpt-ink); }
  .row-n { font-size: .75rem; color: var(--rpt-ink-2); text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
</style>
